#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078213 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7a2c6bf6-41f2-5f8d-8799-5664ba941dc8" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Integral Over an Interval of Length Zero"}] :question/id #uuid "c02d2000-3fef-59e4-9127-84500ef83eb8" :question/math-academy-id "e-418" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int_\\pi^\\pi \\sqrt{\\sin x}\\,\\textrm d x$." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the lower and upper limits are the same. So, the integral evaluates to zero:\n\n$$\n\\int_\\pi^\\pi \\sqrt{\\sin x}\\,\\textrm d x = 0\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078214 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff2f1a9d-2b0e-5a18-98d5-4f49f6f1527b" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Integral Given Another Integral With Reversed Limits of Integration"}] :question/id #uuid "c7a90d29-e8b9-5a06-94f7-776eb254cc52" :question/math-academy-id "e-419" :question/problem "Given that ${\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{6} \\, f(x) \\; \\textrm d x = -54}$, what is the value of\n${\\displaystyle \\int_{6}^{0} \\bigg(\\dfrac 1 3 f(x) + 1 \\bigg) \\; \\textrm d x}$?" :question/worked-solution "Let's reverse the limits of integration on the known result:\n\n$$\n∫_{6}^{0}f(x)dx = - ∫_{0}^{6}f(x)dx = 54\n$$\n\nNow, we can split up the given integral using the sum rule and factor out the constants. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{6}^{0}(\\frac{1}{3}f(x) + 1)dx &= \\frac{1}{3}∫_{6}^{0}f(x)dx + ∫_{6}^{0}1dx \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3} \\cdot 54 + x \\mid _{6}^{0} \\\\\n&= 18 + (0 - 6) \\\\\n&= 12\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078218 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9693b76e-ba77-5314-bfbf-508b25bb1072" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Definite Integral Given Integrals Over Adjacent Intervals"}] :question/id #uuid "a85f51c5-601b-52ec-82dd-0b611842d3d1" :question/math-academy-id "e-420" :question/problem "If $\\displaystyle \\int_7^{10}f(x)\\,\\textrm d x = 18$ and $\\displaystyle \\int_0^{7}f(x)\\,\\textrm d x = -6$ then what is $\\displaystyle \\int_0^{10} \\big(2f(x)+1 \\big) \\,\\textrm d x$?" :question/worked-solution "From the adjacent intervals rule, we know that\n\n$$\n\\int_0^{7}f(x)\\,\\textrm d x + \\displaystyle \\int_7^{10}f(x)\\,\\textrm d x = \\displaystyle \\int_0^{10}f(x)\\,\\textrm d x\n$$\n\nPlugging in the known information into the above gives the following:\n\n$$\n-6 + 18 = ∫_{0}^{10}f(x)dx; 12 = ∫_{0}^{10}f(x)dx\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute the desired integral and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{0}^{10}(2f(x) + 1)dx &= 2∫_{0}^{10}f(x)dx + ∫_{0}^{10}dx \\\\\n&= 2(12) + x \\mid _{0}^{10} \\\\\n&= 24 + (10 - 0) \\\\\n&= 34\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078227 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1d752aac-6187-595d-8818-b0179fd7380e" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Definite Integral Using Multiple Properties of Definite Integrals"}] :question/id #uuid "3d925b89-dc4d-59e0-bf00-3a3ca90b5169" :question/math-academy-id "e-421" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x)$ is a function such that ${\\displaystyle \\int_4^1 2 \\, f(x) \\, \\textrm dx = -1}$\nand ${\\displaystyle \\int_{1}^{-1} f(x) \\, \\textrm dx = 2}$. What is the value of ${\\displaystyle \\int_{-1}^4 f(x) \\, \\textrm dx}$?" :question/worked-solution "Let's simplify the first given integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{4}^{1}2f(x)dx &= -1 \\\\\n2∫_{4}^{1}f(x)dx &= -1 \\\\\n- 2∫_{1}^{4}f(x)dx &= -1 \\\\\n∫_{1}^{4}f(x)dx &= \\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe second integral can be rewritten as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{1}^{-1}f(x)dx &= 2 \\\\\n- ∫_{-1}^{1}f(x)dx &= 2 \\\\\n∫_{-1}^{1}f(x)dx &= -2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, from the adjacent intervals rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{-1}^{4}f(x)dx &= ∫_{-1}^{1}f(x)dx + ∫_{1}^{4}f(x)dx \\\\\n&=-2 + \\frac{1}{2} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{3}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078951 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff2f1a9d-2b0e-5a18-98d5-4f49f6f1527b" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Integral Given Another Integral With Reversed Limits of Integration"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "840a73f4-cd00-5060-8970-125b44881219" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076918} {:answer/id #uuid "f9b6c093-2259-5ba4-89ca-aee6d959ede3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076919} {:answer/id #uuid "13dfae14-52db-5679-a8bc-82045064ac63" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "17" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076920} {:answer/id #uuid "4dc15368-5973-5d55-8088-439c77a8bdc2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-17" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076921} {:answer/id #uuid "7aa99ec8-79af-5880-8a3b-caf7bd4400c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076922}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "13dfae14-52db-5679-a8bc-82045064ac63" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "17" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076920} :answer-field/id #uuid "161475ce-a08f-5735-b906-871a8bac44fb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076917}] :question/id #uuid "9bed53a1-bdd6-5f20-ab7f-4c6204732818" :question/math-academy-id "q-49244" :question/problem "Given that $∫_{5}^{2}f(x)dx =-4$, calculate\n\n$∫_{2}^{5}(2f(x) + 3)dx$."}]}
